'''tubbing-wedge''' (tub′ing-wej), ''n.'' A wedge of yellow pine, about 4 inches in length. {{smaller|Wedges of this kind are driven in between the joints of tubbing in order to make them water-tight.}}

'''tubbish''' (tub′ish), ''a.'' [< ''tub'' + ''-ish''<sup>1</sup>.] Like a tub; tubby; round and fat.
{{smaller block|He was a short, round, large-faced, ''tubbish'' sort of man.
{{right|''Dickens'', Sketches, Characters, vii.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>You look for men whose heads are rather ''tubbish'',
Or drum-like, better formed for sound than sense.</poem>
{{right|''Wolcot'' (Peter Pindar), Works, p. 136. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''tubby''' (tub′i), ''a.'' [< ''tub'' + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Tub-shaped; round like a tub or barrel.
{{smaller block|We had seen him coming up to Covent Garden in his green chaise-cart with the fat, ''tubby'' little horse.
{{right|''Dickens'', Sketches, Scenes, vi.}}}}
'''2.''' Having a sound like that of an empty tub when struck; sounding dull and without resonance: applied to stringed musical instruments.

'''tub-drubber†''' (tub′drub″ėr), ''n.'' A tub-thumper or tub-preacher. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|Business and poetry agree as ill together as faith and reason: which two latter, as has been judiciously observ’d by the fam’d ''tub-drubber'' of Covent Garden, can never be brought to set their horses together.
{{right|''Tom Brown'', Works, III. 198. (''Davies.'')}}}}

